    "limiter": {
      "name": "Limiter",
      "description": "Keeps peaks at or below `ceiling`, whatever comes in: the last stage before an output or a render, or a way to make a track louder without clipping. Mono or stereo.\n\n- `ceiling` (dB): the highest level that gets out.\n- `release` (seconds): how quickly the limiter lets go after a peak. Short releases are louder but can pump or distort low notes; longer ones are smoother.\n- `gain_reduction` (output, dB): how far the signal is being turned down right now (0 or negative).\n\nThe limiter looks 5 ms ahead, so it can start turning down before a peak arrives instead of letting its first few milliseconds through. That 5 ms is reported as latency, so the engine delays parallel paths to stay in step.\n\nInside: a Lookahead splits the input into `early` (as it comes in) and `out` (5 ms later). A peak Envelope Follower on `early` with an instant attack finds each peak 5 ms before it is heard, and a Smooth spreads the rise of that level over about a millisecond so the gain ramps down instead of jumping. By the time a peak is heard, the first level has already begun to release and sags a little, so a second instant follower on `out` catches whatever is left over, and a Math node keeps the louder of the two levels. A Gain Computer with a ratio of 1000:1 and a hard knee, its threshold set 0.05 dB under the ceiling so that even a signal 50 dB too hot stays under, turns any level over the ceiling into the gain change for the Gain on `out`.",
      "version": 1,
      "author": "Audio Constructor",
      "tags": [
        "dynamics",
        "limiter",
        "mastering",
        "mixing"
      ],
      "source": "shipped:limiter",
      "inputs": [
        {
          "name": "in",
          "type": "audio",
          "targets": [
            "1:in"
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "ceiling",
          "type": "float",
          "unit": "db",
          "default": -1.0,
          "range": [
            -24.0,
            0.0
          ],
          "targets": [
            "8:a"
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "release",
          "type": "float",
          "unit": "seconds",
          "default": 0.1,
          "range": [
            0.01,
            1.0
          ],
          "targets": [
            "2:release",
            "4:release"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "out",
          "type": "audio",
          "source": "7:out"
        },
        {
          "name": "gain_reduction",
          "type": "float",
          "source": "6:gain_reduction"
        }
      ],
      "graph": {
        "nodes": [
          {
            "id": "1",
            "kind": "lookahead",
            "config": {
              "time": 0.005
            },
            "ui": {
              "pos": [
                0.0,
                0.0
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "2",
            "kind": "envelope_follower",
            "params": {
              "attack": 0.0,
              "mode": "peak"
            },
            "ui": {
              "pos": [
                220.0,
                0.0
              ],
              "label": "Early detector"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "3",
            "kind": "smooth",
            "params": {
              "fall": 0.0,
              "mode": "exponential",
              "rise": 0.0007
            },
            "ui": {
              "pos": [
                440.0,
                0.0
              ],
              "label": "Ramp"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "4",
            "kind": "envelope_follower",
            "params": {
              "attack": 0.0,
              "mode": "peak"
            },
            "ui": {
              "pos": [
                440.0,
                160.0
              ],
              "label": "Catch"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "5",
            "kind": "math",
            "params": {
              "op": "max"
            },
            "ui": {
              "pos": [
                660.0,
                0.0
              ],
              "label": "Louder"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "6",
            "kind": "gain_computer",
            "params": {
              "knee": 0.0,
              "mode": "compress",
              "ratio": 1000.0
            },
            "ui": {
              "pos": [
                880.0,
                0.0
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "7",
            "kind": "gain",
            "ui": {
              "pos": [
                1100.0,
                160.0
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "8",
            "kind": "math",
            "params": {
              "b": 0.05,
              "op": "subtract"
            },
            "ui": {
              "pos": [
                660.0,
                -160.0
              ],
              "label": "Margin"
            }
          }
        ],
        "wires": [
          {
            "from": "8:out",
            "to": "6:threshold"
          },
          {
            "from": "1:early",
            "to": "2:in"
          },
          {
            "from": "1:out",
            "to": "4:in"
          },
          {
            "from": "1:out",
            "to": "7:in"
          },
          {
            "from": "2:level_db",
            "to": "3:in"
          },
          {
            "from": "3:out",
            "to": "5:a"
          },
          {
            "from": "4:level_db",
            "to": "5:b"
          },
          {
            "from": "5:out",
            "to": "6:level"
          },
          {
            "from": "6:gain_reduction",
            "to": "7:gain"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "plate-reverb": {
      "name": "Plate Reverb",
      "description": "A dense, bright plate reverb: a burst of echoes that thickens at once into a smooth, shimmering tail. Mono or stereo in (summed to mono, as a plate is), wide stereo out.\n\n- `in`: the signal.\n- `predelay` (s): a gap before the reverb starts, which keeps the dry attack clear; 0.01 to 0.04 s for vocals and snares.\n- `decay` (s): how long the tail takes to fall by 60 dB (its RT60).\n- `damping` (0 to 1): how much faster the highs die away than the lows; 0 is a bright metal plate, 0.5 a darker, softer one.\n- `mix` (0 to 1): 0 is dry, 1 only the reverb. The blend is equal-power, so the loudness holds steady; 0.2 to 0.35 sits a sound in a mix, 1 is for a reverb on a send.\n- `out`: the stereo result.\n\nInside: the figure-eight tank from Jon Dattorro's \"Effect Design, Part 1: Reverberator and Other Filters\" (JAES, 1997), with his delay lengths converted to seconds. The input is summed to mono, delayed by `predelay`, and smeared by four input Allpasses (4.8, 3.6, 12.7, and 9.3 ms). It then enters two tank halves, each an Allpass whose delay wobbles slightly (a slow sine Oscillator through a Map, to keep the tail from ringing), a feedforward Comb used as a delay line with the damping lowpass and the decay gain, a second Allpass, and another Comb as a delay with the decay gain. Each half feeds the other: the left half goes straight into the right, and the right comes back to the left through a Feedback node, the loop's only cycle (its one block of delay barely lengthens a loop of about 0.73 s). The decay gain comes from `decay` through Math and Convert: the loop applies it four times per trip, so each is −60 dB × 0.19 s ÷ `decay`. As in the original, each output reads the opposite half: the left channel mixes the outputs of the right half's four stages, and the right channel the left half's. A Mid/Side encode and decode at width 1.6 then widens the pair until the two sides are uncorrelated, and an equal-power Crossfade blends that with the dry input.",
      "version": 1,
      "author": "Audio Constructor",
      "tags": [
        "reverb",
        "space",
        "plate",
        "stereo",
        "effect"
      ],
      "source": "shipped:plate-reverb",
      "inputs": [
        {
          "name": "in",
          "type": "audio",
          "targets": [
            "1:in",
            "26:a"
          ]
        },
